{"title": "A Mathematical Model of Historical Semantics and the Grouping of Word Meanings into Concepts", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1162/0891201054223995", "provider": "crossref", "query_lane": "scripture_math", "query": "Bible computational linguistics mathematical", "retrieved_at": "2026-08-25T11:18:11.926816+00:00", "authors": ["Martin C. Cooper"], "year": 2005, "abstract": "A statistical analysis of polysemy in sixteen English and French dictionaries has revealed that, in each dictionary, the number of senses per word has a near-exponential distribution. A probabilistic model of historical semantics is presented which explains this distribution. This mathematical model also provides a means of estimating the average number of distinct concepts per word, which was found to be considerably less than the average number of senses listed per word. The grouping of word senses into concepts is based on whether they could inspire the same new senses (by metaphor, metonymy, etc.), that is, their potential future rather than their history.", "venue": "Computational Linguistics", "doi": "10.1162/0891201054223995", "cited_by_count": 5, "open_access_url": "", "provider_id": "10.1162/0891201054223995", "source_type": "scholarly_metadata", "math_signals": ["mathematical", "model", "semantics", "statistical"], "theology_signals": [], "lane": "BACKGROUND", "relevance_score": 48, "evidence_grade": "C", "screening_status": "BACKGROUND_ONLY", "content_hash": "b4971f23a99d82f2fd1a120f559c05e4c9db7e8a09c8909c46d1036e61cf9f07"}
{"title": "Language Mathematics and Mathematics Language, Reading from Computational Linguistics", "url": "https://doi.org/10.58205/ml.v1i1.140", "provider": "crossref", "query_lane": "scripture_math", "query": "Bible computational linguistics mathematical", "retrieved_at": "2026-08-25T11:18:11.926816+00:00", "authors": ["Salim Mezhoud"], "year": 2021, "abstract": "The language of mathematics is the system used by mathematicians to communicate mathematical ideas among themselves. This language consists of a substrate of some natural language using technical terms and grammatical conventions that are peculiar to mathematical discourse, supplemented by a highly specialized symbolic notation for mathematical formulas. mathematical characterizations of various notions of linguistic complexity include also computational linguistics, philosophical logic, knowledge representation as a branch of artificial intelligence, theoretical computer science, and computational psychology. Mathematical linguistics has initially served as a foundation for computational linguistics, though its research agenda of designing machines to simulate natural language understanding is clearly more applied. Inductive methods have gained the upper hand in applied computational linguistics The question is whether mathematics is a language, or that language is mathematical, and how computational linguistics employs language as mathematics.", "venue": "Mathematical Linguistics", "doi": "10.58205/ml.v1i1.140", "cited_by_count": 0, "open_access_url": "", "provider_id": "10.58205/ml.v1i1.140", "source_type": "scholarly_metadata", "math_signals": ["logic", "mathematical", "mathematics"], "theology_signals": ["sin"], "lane": "THEOLOGICAL_BRIDGE", "relevance_score": 48, "evidence_grade": "C", "screening_status": "SCREEN", "content_hash": "ef7f8b3bb4a5914a03df186c6110a68739bfaa3be7fe9650b83785107ed86544"}
{"title": "Computational Linguistic Analysis of the Biblical Text", "url": "https://doi.org/10.11647/obp.0358.05", "provider": "crossref", "query_lane": "scripture_math", "query": "biblical text network analysis graph theory", "retrieved_at": "2026-08-25T11:18:09.295524+00:00", "authors": ["Willem Th. van Peursen"], "year": 2023, "abstract": "Historical linguistics enjoys a venerable history among the many subfields of linguistic study. Many of the tools employed in histori-cal linguistics, as well as some of its theoretical concepts, are well-suited for biblical studies which engage in the ancient languages of the Bible. Knowledge about the kinds of language change common to many of the world’s languages can be useful as one type of evi-dence for the periodisation of biblical books. Additionally, knowledge about the external linguistic influences that shaped the biblical languages, as well as their prior histories (both Semitic and Indo-European) provide a helpful context for studying many syn-chronic aspects of the texts of Scripture. Furthermore, text-critical judgments about the biblical text can be strengthened when in-formed by knowledge of language change across the manuscript tradition. In short, historical linguistics offers a number of unique insights for biblical scholars engaging in the study of the biblical languages. Key words: Historical linguistics, diachronic, sound change, anal-ogy, Indo-European, Semitic", "venue": "Semitic Languages and Cultures; Linguistic Theory and the Biblical Text", "doi": "10.11647/obp.0358.05", "cited_by_count": 1, "open_access_url": "", "provider_id": "10.11647/obp.0358.05", "source_type": "scholarly_metadata", "math_signals": [], "theology_signals": ["bible", "biblical", "scripture"], "lane": "BACKGROUND", "relevance_score": 42, "evidence_grade": "C", "screening_status": "BACKGROUND_ONLY", "content_hash": "37f2e7a937707ccf41a457b6d43a5f5e50ca34e3f5ee88d0b924f70fcf275349"}
{"title": "Computational Linguistics Approach", "url": "https://doi.org/10.58205/ml.v2i1.155", "provider": "crossref", "query_lane": "scripture_math", "query": "Bible computational linguistics mathematical", "retrieved_at": "2026-08-25T11:18:11.926816+00:00", "authors": ["Aicha Bara"], "year": 2022, "abstract": "This article aims to explain the field of computational linguistics and its specializations، its interest in computational modeling of natural language، and the study of computational approaches appropriate to linguistic questions. In general، computational linguistics draws on linguistics، computer science، artificial intelligence، mathematics، logic، philosophy، cognitive science، cognitive psychology، psycholinguistics، anthropology، and neuroscience، among others.", "venue": "Mathematical Linguistics", "doi": "10.58205/ml.v2i1.155", "cited_by_count": 0, "open_access_url": "", "provider_id": "10.58205/ml.v2i1.155", "source_type": "scholarly_metadata", "math_signals": ["logic", "mathematics", "model"], "theology_signals": [], "lane": "BACKGROUND", "relevance_score": 42, "evidence_grade": "C", "screening_status": "BACKGROUND_ONLY", "content_hash": "f7cf39908687ecc47992680b26f10f1c26c8b3774a9d069db4283daa7c887ead"}
{"title": "The Vaiman Model: Synchronizing Biblical Chronology and Ancient Near East Archaeology via the 364-Day Calendar and Jubilee Intercalation", "url": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6740499", "provider": "crossref", "query_lane": "scripture_math", "query": "biblical chronology mathematical model", "retrieved_at": "2026-08-25T11:18:13.110894+00:00", "authors": ["Daniel Vaiman"], "year": 2026, "abstract": "The 364-day calendar, extensively documented in the Dead Sea Scrolls and Second Temple literature, has historically been dismissed as an impractical, non-functional system due to its inherent solar drift of approximately 1.24 days per year. This paper proposes the \"Vaiman Model,\" a systemic and mathematical framework that resolves this drift through a 50-day intercalation cycle occurring every Jubilee (50 years). By applying this deterministic calendar and the principle of \"System Uptime\" (counting only periods of national sovereignty) to the biblical narrative, the model achieves an absolute synchronization with the archaeological record. The results place the Exodus at 1550 BCE, aligning with the destruction of Jericho IV and Tel Haror, and synchronize the Davidic-Solomonic era with Carbon-14 data from Khirbet Qeiyafa (1020-980 BCE) and Tel Megiddo (967-943 BCE). The model suggests that the biblical counting of the Omer and the Jubilee were not merely ritualistic but functioned as a decentralized, \"antifragile\" socioeconomic operating system.", "venue": "", "doi": "10.2139/ssrn.6740499", "cited_by_count": 0, "open_access_url": "", "provider_id": "10.2139/ssrn.6740499", "source_type": "scholarly_metadata", "math_signals": ["logic", "mathematical", "model"], "theology_signals": ["biblical"], "lane": "THEOLOGICAL_BRIDGE", "relevance_score": 42, "evidence_grade": "C", "screening_status": "SCREEN", "content_hash": "86f7b62a0d5a0d00e1e0b9651bc97160635dfb338f129908698338ee412eae07"}
